"A Git history graph shows commits, parent relationships, and references without mistaking branch names for separate copies of history.\n\nUse when: Explain merge ancestry and divergence. Review which commits a branch or tag can reach.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is future scheduling or deployment status.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes identify commits using short verified object IDs. Directed edges consistently represent parent ancestry under a stated orientation. Branch and tag labels point to referenced commits rather than enclosing whole lanes.\n\nBuild: Choose repository references and an inclusion boundary. Extract parent relationships from Git rather than reconstructing them from dates. Draw merge parents and label relevant refs. Use separate lanes for diverging paths and keep ref labels clear of merge bends.\n\nCheck: Every shown parent edge matches the actual commit graph. Commit timestamps do not override ancestry ordering. A branch pointer is distinguished from the historical commits it reaches. Merge lines and reference labels do not overlap commit identifiers.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a branch lane permanently belongs to one branch name. Treating cherry-picked commits as identical objects because patches resemble one another.\n\nExample: Feature diverges from Main, adds two commits, then merges through a two-parent merge commit.\n\nAlternatives: Artifact promotion diagram, Module dependency graph."
